pkg/proc, pkg/terminal: name the inlined function in hits; returns follow the condition

A breakpoint on a function inlined into another, such as a method
promoted through an embedded struct and inlined into the compiler's
wrappers ((*shared).AddReadonlyTXID in (*array).AddReadonlyTXID), stops
in the caller's code, and every hit line named the caller: a tracepoint
on (*shared).AddReadonlyTXID printed "(*array).AddReadonlyTXID(...)",
which reads as a hit of another function. api.Thread.Inlined now carries
the function inlined where the thread stopped, and hit and stop lines
name it, followed by the function it was inlined into:

	> goroutine(1): [a] main.(*shared).AddReadonlyTXID(...) (inlined in main.(*hashMap).AddReadonlyTXID)

In the JSON output, "func" is the inlined function and "inlined_in" the
caller.

The breakpoints a tracepoint sets on its function's returns had no
condition, so a conditional tracepoint printed a return line for every
call, also those whose entry did not pass the condition. A return
breakpoint now belongs to its tracepoint (api.Breakpoint.TraceReturnOf),
which records for each call, by goroutine and frame, whether its entry
stopped, and the return stops only if it did, so returns follow the
condition, also for recursive calls and many goroutines.

Both are generic fixes for upstream Delve; the JSON field is for the
output mode of 0003.

